# Line endings, and why this file has to exist.
#
# This repository publishes receipts that hash a file's raw bytes, and a line ending is a
# byte. With `core.autocrlf` true and no rules here, the working tree on Windows held CRLF
# while the repository and the Linux runner held LF, so every one of those hashes meant one
# thing on the machine that wrote it and something else everywhere else.
#
# It was not a theory. tests/fixtures/agent_runs.json is 85137 bytes on Windows and contains
# 2020 CRLF pairs. Hashed as it sat on disk it is
# a162fbc3dcfd4e813ff934958909453b53d29fe973e4c3f3640aa6d791c2b7ae, which is the value the
# committed receipt carried. Hashed with LF endings it is
# e9759ff65d8d5ba2512322b29494357d5173492d6dac3a4dcde2ebe1b392c995, which is the value CI
# computed, and CI was right. Twenty consecutive runs failed on that class of mismatch across
# four receipts, while every check passed locally, because locally the file and the receipt
# were wrong together.
#
# tests/test_grounding_parity.py hashes a `.py` file, not a fixture, so this reaches source
# and not only data. There is no subset of this repository where the rule can be narrower.
#
# One meaning per hash, on every machine. That is the whole content of this file.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binaries are never converted. A normalised waterfall image is a corrupted waterfall image,
# and these are the four types in the tree that are not text.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.webp binary
*.mp4 binary

# The Windows launchers keep CRLF, and they keep it on every platform rather than per
# checkout. A `.cmd` is read by cmd.exe, which is entitled to expect its own line ending, and
# pinning the ending in both directions is what keeps a hash of one reproducible anyway.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
